---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import BandTable from "../../components/BandTable.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import { courseModel, indicators, sites, pct } from "../../lib/model";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

const recipe = courseModel.recipe;
const sens = courseModel.sensitivity;
const recipeRows: { label: string; key: keyof typeof recipe.baseline; fmt?: (v: unknown) => string }[] = [
  { label: "Domestic demand (L/person/day)", key: "demand" },
  { label: "Composting toilet", key: "composting_toilet", fmt: (v) => (v ? "yes" : "no") },
  { label: "Share of greywater reused", key: "greywater", fmt: (v) => `${Number(v) * 100} %` },
  { label: "Share of growable area in production", key: "production", fmt: (v) => `${Number(v) * 100} %` },
  { label: "Yield vs 1,500 kcal/m²/yr reference", key: "yield_mult", fmt: (v) => `${v}×` },
  { label: "Share of excreted N recovered", key: "n_recovery", fmt: (v) => `${Number(v) * 100} %` },
  { label: "Aquaponic grow bed", key: "aquaponic_m2", fmt: (v) => (v ? `${v} m²` : "none") },
  { label: "Added roof catchment", key: "extra_catchment", fmt: (v) => (v ? `+${Number(v) * 100} % of roof` : "none") },
  { label: "New storage spend (tank shells)", key: "storage_budget", fmt: (v) => `$${Number(v).toLocaleString("en-AU")}` },
];
const LV = ["baseline", "competent", "excellent"] as const;

const worstSpread = Object.entries(sens.spreads)
  .map(([k, v]) => ({ key: k, ...v }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.worst.spread - a.worst.spread);
const nameOf = (k: string) => indicators.find((i) => i.key === k)?.name ?? k;
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Method: indicators, bands and what they are not"
  description="How this course turns a design into evidence: five capped indicators, per-site reference bands built from three constructed designs, and a clear statement of what the method does not establish."
  wide
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <h2>What is measured</h2>
  <p>
    Five indicators, all capped at 100 %, all counted as <em>used</em> rather than
    produced, and all net of imports. That last rule is the one that does the work:
    a system running on bought-in feed or carted water scores as what it is.
  </p>

  <Figure caption="The five banded indicators and their definitions." basis="model estimate">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Indicator</th><th scope="col">Definition</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          indicators.map((i) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{i.name}</th>
              <td>{i.definition}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <h3>Water closure never appears alone</h3>
  <p>
    Water closure is on-site water used divided by all water used. Water that is
    never used enters neither term — so a design can post a very high closure
    figure by leaving its garden dry. Alice Springs' excellent reference design
    reaches {pct(sites[1].bands.water_closure.excellent)} water closure with{" "}
    {pct(sites[1].bands.garden_water_satisfaction.excellent)} garden water
    satisfaction. <strong>The two are published together everywhere, without
    exception.</strong>
  </p>

  <h3>Waste-stream recovery is deliberately not banded</h3>
  <p>
    Every banded indicator takes its thresholds from three constructed reference
    designs run through the model. There is no reference calculation for
    waste-stream recovery, so a band for it would have nothing behind it. It is
    assessed from your submitted mass balances against the rubric instead. Saying
    so is better than inventing a number and calling it a measurement.
  </p>

  <h2>Reference bands, and what they borrow from NatHERS</h2>
  <p>
    NatHERS could not use one national energy target across Australian climates
    because a single figure "would bias results", so it sets climate-specific star
    bands. This course borrows the <em>idea</em> and states plainly that it is not
    the method: NatHERS derives its bands from a population of housing
    simulations, while these come from <strong>three constructed reference
    designs</strong> — baseline, competent and excellent — run through the course's
    screening model at each site.
  </p>

  <Figure caption="The four bands." basis="course assumption">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Band</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Below the baseline reference design</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Between baseline and competent</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Between competent and excellent</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>At or beyond excellent</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <h3>The reference recipe, identical at every site</h3>
  <p>
    One recipe, five climates. Everything that differs between sites' thresholds
    comes from the climate and the site inventory, never from a different design
    being used as the yardstick.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Reference-design recipe, version 5. Storage spend and added catchment changed in September 2026 after the designs were costed against the full price schedule; see the costing page."
    basis="course assumption"
  >
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Parameter</th><th scope="col">Baseline</th><th scope="col">Competent</th><th scope="col">Excellent</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          recipeRows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.label}</th>
              {LV.map((l) => <td>{r.fmt ? r.fmt(recipe[l][r.key]) : String(recipe[l][r.key])}</td>)}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <h3>Your site's thresholds</h3>
  <p>
    Each cell reads <strong>baseline / competent / excellent</strong>, as percentages.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Reference-band thresholds by site, in per cent. No excellent value reaches 100 % under this recipe, so band 4 is open at every site."
    basis="model estimate"
  >
    <BandTable />
  </Figure>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>Rounding.</strong> Bands are evaluated against the model's unrounded
    reference values. The percentages above are a rounded <em>display</em> of those
    values, not the thresholds themselves — a design at 5.51 % against a threshold
    displayed as 5.5 % is above it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-stable-are-the-bands">How stable are the bands?</h2>
  <p>
    The three reference designs were re-run under eleven perturbations of the
    model's most uncertain constants — reference yield at ±33 %, domestic demand at
    120 and 180 L/person/day, runoff coefficient, crop coefficient, feed conversion
    ratio and tank cost. For each, the question is where the <em>competent</em> design
    lands inside its site's baseline-to-excellent band. If the method is fair,
    that position should be similar at every site.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Worst-case spread of the competent design's band position across the five sites, by indicator. A spread of 0 would mean the competent design sits in exactly the same relative place at every site."
    basis="model estimate"
  >
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Indicator</th>
          <th scope="col">Worst spread</th>
          <th scope="col">Under which perturbation</th>
          <th scope="col">Next worst</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          worstSpread.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{nameOf(r.key)}</th>
              <td>{r.worst.spread}</td>
              <td>{r.worst.perturbation}</td>
              <td>{r.second_worst.spread}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <p>
    Read that honestly. Food energy and protein are stable. <strong>Garden water
    satisfaction is the least stable indicator in the set</strong>, and both it and
    water closure have their worst case under the same perturbation, domestic
    demand at 120 L/person/day. Excluding that one case, no indicator spreads by
    more than 0.17.
  </p>
  <p>
    Garden water satisfaction was only brought inside this test in September 2026.
    Before then the stability claim covered four indicators and was quietly assumed
    to extend to the fifth. It does not, and the figure above is what that
    assumption was hiding.
  </p>

  <h3>What the bands do not establish</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>They are not grades.</strong> A band is performance evidence inside the
      marking criteria, contributing to <em>Calculations</em> and <em>Loop
      integration</em>. Nothing converts a band into a mark.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>They do not prove equal grading outcomes.</strong> The testing above shows
      three constructed designs behaving consistently. It says nothing directly
      about how real student designs, which vary in ways the model does not
      represent, will distribute.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Narrow bands are noisy.</strong> Alice Springs' food energy band spans about
      three percentage points end to end. That is why bands are ordinal — four
      levels — rather than a continuous score.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>They must be recomputed whenever a course constant changes.</strong> Household
      demand, allowances, the price schedule, model coefficients or the reference
      recipe: any of them, and every threshold on this page is republished.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The screening model behind all of it</h2>
  <p>
    Monthly, transparent, and built only from published formulas so that a student
    can reproduce any number by hand. It has no drought years, no pests, no soil
    types and no labour. Water temperature is approximated by air temperature.
    Food yield rests on a single reference figure of 1,500 kcal/m²/year inside a
    published range of 500 to 3,000.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a screening model, not a validated engineering design, and its outputs
    are not predictions of real yields. Everything it produces on this site is
    labelled with its basis.
  </p>

  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Costing and energy" href={withBase("/method/costing/")}>
      <p>The price schedule, the bill-of-materials rules, and exactly which loads count against 5 kWh a day.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Worked comparison" href={withBase("/method/comparison/")}>
      <p>One design idea — an extra 20 kL of storage — taken through all five sites, with the answers it gets.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Datasets" href={withBase("/method/datasets/")}>
      <p>The synthetic troubleshooting data the assignments use, and what it is and is not.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="The five clients" href={withBase("/clients/")}>
      <p>Parameters, inventory, and the strengths and constraints no indicator scores.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  th[scope="row"] {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .note {
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
